KNOWNBUG libstdcxx
main.cpp
--cpp11
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ rethrown: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^\*\* 1 of \d+ failed
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
--
std::current_exception, std::rethrow_exception and exception_ptr's
_M_addref/_M_release are defined in libstdc++'s compiled library and have
no model ("no body for callee"), so the rethrow is not modelled.  A model
needs the exception's type tag as well as its object
(__CPROVER_cpp_current_exception and _type in remove_cpp_exceptions), a
reference count that keeps the object alive past its handler, whose exit
now destroys it ([except.throw]/4, [propagation]/9), and a rethrow that
installs the pair as the in-flight exception.
